        {
          "id": "SP_CHAPERIA_MULTISPINOSA_GORDON_1984",
          "name": "Chaperia multispinosa Gordon, 1984",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Chaperia",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "Colony encrusting, multiserial, up to c. 12 mm longest diameter, pale brown. Zooids variable in shape and size, moderately large, quincuncial, ranging from subquadrangular (diamond-shaped) to irregularly pentagonal, distal margin arcuate, much raised, lateral margins of zooid typically diverging outwards proximally, straight, proximal end truncate, concavely arcuate or V-shaped depending on position in colony; length: width ratio variable, longer than wide (ZL: ZW 1.17) on average in more distal reproductive parts of colony, but tending to be wider than long (ZL: ZW 0.89) in proximal half of colony. Interzooidal boundaries marked by thin lines in moderately deep furrows. Cryptocystal frontal wall sloping downwards to proximal margin, rising to level of orificial rim, surface unevenly granular-tubercular, no narrow extension of cryptocyst around opesia distally. ZL 290 ‒ 807 (513) μm, ZW 347 ‒ 850 (463) μm, n = 50. Mean ZL: ZW ratio 1.10. Opesia variable in length and width, mostly wider than long but in some zooids can be as long as wide or longer, mostly transversely suboval, distal rim and lateral rim raised, proximolateral corners of opesia rim often elevated as small lappet-like crest or nodule, proximal margin narrow, smooth moderately to mostly gently concave. Frontal rims of occlusor laminae not very thin, mostly gently curving, sometimes straight, widely separated distally.",
